.\" Manual page for sweep
.Dd September 28, 2026
.Dt SWEEP 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm sweep
.Nd clean, safe, and transparent macOS application uninstaller
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl -version
.Op Fl h | -help
.Nm
.Cm list
.Op Fl -json
.Nm
.Cm scan
.Ar app
.Op Fl -json
.Nm
.Cm remove
.Ar app
.Op Fl -dry-run
.Op Fl y | -yes
.Op Fl -permanent
.Op Fl -include-low-confidence
.Op Fl -json
.Nm
.Cm doctor
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
is a command-line uninstaller engineered specifically for macOS.
Dragging an application bundle (.app) to the macOS Trash leaves behind
orphaned caches, preferences, sandboxed containers, saved states,
application support files, launch agents, and background helper daemons.
.Pp
.Nm
locates all associated leftovers using data-driven heuristics,
verifies them against strict system safety guards, displays all
candidate file paths to the user, and moves them to the native macOS
Trash preserving Finder recovery.
.Sh TARGET RESOLUTION
The
.Ar app
argument accepted by
.Cm scan
and
.Cm remove
can be any of the following:
.Bl -bullet -compact
.It
An application name (e.g.,
.Qq Slack ,
.Qq "Google Chrome" )
.It
A reverse-DNS bundle identifier (e.g.,
.Qq com.tinyspeck.slackmacgap )
.It
An absolute or relative filesystem path to an application bundle (e.g.,
.Pa /Applications/Slack.app )
.El
.Sh SUBCOMMANDS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm list Op Fl -json
Scans
.Pa /Applications
and
.Pa ~/Applications
for installed applications and displays their display names, bundle
identifiers, and bundle sizes.
.It Cm scan Ar app Op Fl -json
Resolves the target application, queries installer receipts via
.Xr pkgutil 1 ,
and scans all standard user and system library domains for leftovers.
Displays items grouped by location along with byte sizes, confidence
levels, and matching rationale.
.Sy This subcommand deletes nothing.
.It Cm remove Op cache Op Ar app Op flags
When invoked with
.Qq cache
(e.g.,
.Cm sweep remove cache
or
.Cm sweep remove cache Ar Safari ) ,
scans and purges application cache directories (including
.Pa ~/Library/Caches ,
Darwin system user caches, and sandbox container caches) while preserving the application binary, user preferences, and documents.
Core system applications (such as Safari and Finder) are permitted for cache purging.
All cache deletions require administrator authentication
.Pq Xr sudo 8 .
When invoked with an application name directly, performs a complete uninstallation, unloads related LaunchAgents or LaunchDaemons with
.Xr launchctl 1 ,
and safely moves selected items to the macOS Trash.
.It Cm doctor
Runs diagnostic checks verifying Full Disk Access (FDA), macOS version
compatibility (macOS 13.0+ required), User Trash permissions, System
Integrity Protection (SIP), and the configuration history store.
.El
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl -dry-run
Simulates the removal process.
Displays every file path that would be touched without modifying or
moving any files on disk.
.It Fl y , -yes
Skips interactive confirmation prompts and automatically selects all
High and Medium confidence leftover candidates for removal.
.It Fl -permanent
Permanently deletes items from the filesystem using direct removal
rather than moving them to the macOS Trash.
.Sy Use with caution:
items deleted with this flag cannot be recovered via Finder.
.It Fl -include-low-confidence
Includes low-confidence candidates (such as generic vendor-prefixed
directories) in the removal plan.
By default, low-confidence items are excluded to prevent accidental
removal of shared vendor resources.
.It Fl -json
Outputs structured JSON data instead of human-readable colored
terminal output.
Useful for script automation and machine consumption.
.It Fl -version
Displays the current version of
.Nm .
.It Fl h , -help
Displays command-line usage information.
.El
.Sh SAFETY INVARIANTS
.Nm
enforces defense-in-depth safety invariants to prevent system damage:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Sy System Denylist
Operations involving root and system paths are strictly blocked:
.Pa / ,
.Pa /System ,
.Pa /usr ,
.Pa /bin ,
.Pa /sbin ,
.Pa /private/etc ,
.Pa /Library/Apple ,
and
.Pa /Library/SystemExtensions .
.It Sy Credential & Identity Protection
A zero-touch policy is enforced on user authentication databases.
.Pa ~/Library/Keychains ,
.Pa ~/Library/AuthenticationServices ,
and
.Pa ~/Library/IdentityServices
are unconditionally excluded from scanning and deletion.
.It Sy Apple System Application Shield
Applications installed inside sealed system volumes (such as
.Pa /System/Applications
or core components like Finder and Safari) cannot be removed.
Removable user-facing Apple applications in
.Pa /Applications
may be uninstalled with administrator authorization.
.It Sy Symlink Canonicalization
All paths are resolved to their canonical targets using symlink
resolution before validation.
Any symlink attempting to escape into system boundaries is rejected.
.It Sy Native Trash Recovery
By default, files are moved to the Trash via
.Dv FileManager.trashItem ,
preserving APFS metadata and native Finder
.Qq Put Back
restoration.
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Pa ~/.config/sweep/history.json
Audit log containing timestamped records of every removal transaction,
including original file paths, trash destinations, and byte sizes.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Bl -tag -width 4n
.It 0
Successful completion.
.It 1
General error or process execution failure.
.It 2
Command-line usage error (invalid flags or missing arguments).
.It 3
Permission error, System Integrity Protection restriction, or system
application rejection.
.It 4
Target application not found.
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
List installed applications:
.Pp
.Dl $ sweep list
.Pp
Scan an application and view all detected leftover files without deleting anything:
.Pp
.Dl $ sweep scan "Slack"
.Pp
Perform a safe trial run showing exactly what file paths would be deleted:
.Pp
.Dl $ sweep remove "Slack" --dry-run --yes
.Pp
Interactively review and uninstall an application:
.Pp
.Dl $ sweep remove "Slack"
.Pp
Check system permissions and Full Disk Access:
.Pp
.Dl $ sweep doctor
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr launchctl 1 ,
.Xr pkgutil 1
